 group magic labeling\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nLet $G=(V,E)$ be a graph and $Γ$ an Abelian group both of order $n$. A $Γ$-distance magic labeling of $G$ is a bijection $\\ell \\colon V\\rightarrow Γ$ for which there exists $μ\\in Γ$ such that $% \\sum_{x\\in N(v)}\\ell (x)=μ$ for all $v\\in V$, where $N(v)$ is the neighborhood of $v$. Froncek %(\\cite{ref_CicAus}) showed that the Cartesian product $C_m \\square C_n$, $m, n\\geq3$ is a $\\mathbb{Z}_{mn}$-distance magic graph if and only if $mn$ is even. It is also known that if $mn$ is even then $C_m \\square C_n$ has $\\mathbb{Z}_α\\times \\mathcal{A}$-magic labeling for any $α\\equiv 0 \\pmod {{\\rm lcm}(m,n)}$ and any Abelian group $\\mathcal{A}$ of order $mn/α$. %\\cite{ref_CicAus} However, the full characterization of group distance magic Cartesian product of two cycles is still unknown. In the paper we make progress towards the complete solution this problem by proving some necessary conditions. We further prove that for $n$ even the graph $C_{n}\\square C_{n}$ has a $Γ$-distance magic labeling for any Abelian group $Γ$ of order $n^{2}$. Moreover we show that if $m\\neq n$, then there does not exist a $(\\mathbb{Z}_2)^{m+n}$-distance magic labeling of the Cartesian product $C_{2^m} \\square C_{2^{n}}$. We also give necessary and sufficient condition for $C_{m} \\square C_{n}$ with $\\gcd(m,n)=1$ to be $Γ$-distance magic.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGroup distance magic Cartesian product of two cycles (https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.04946)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; It is also known that if $mn$ is even then $C_m \\square C_n$ has $\\mathbb{Z}_{\\alpha}\\times \\mathcal{A}$-magic labeling for any $\\alpha \\equiv 0 \\pmod {{\\rm lcm}(m,n)}$ and any Abelian group $\\mathcal{A}$ of order $mn/\\alpha$. %\\cite{ref_CicAus} However, the full characterization of group distance magic Cartesian product of two cycles is still unknown.\nTitle: Group distance magic Cartesian product of two cycles\nAuthors: Sylwia Cichacz, Dalibor Froncek, Paweł Dyrlaga\nDate: Mon May 13 10:23:02 2019\n\nLet $G=(V,E)$ be a graph and $\\Gamma $ an Abelian group both of order $n$. A $\\Gamma$-distance magic labeling of $G$ is a bijection $\\ell \\colon V\\rightarrow \\Gamma $ for which there exists $\\mu \\in \\Gamma $ such that $% \\sum_{x\\in N(v)}\\ell (x)=\\mu $ for all $v\\in V$, where $N(v)$ is the neighborhood of $v$. Froncek %(\\cite{ref_CicAus}) showed that the Cartesian product $C_m \\square C_n$, $m, n\\geq3$ is a $\\mathbb{Z}_{mn}$-distance magic graph if and only if $mn$ is even. It is also known that if $mn$ is even then $C_m \\square C_n$ has $\\mathbb{Z}_{\\alpha}\\times \\mathcal{A}$-magic labeling for any $\\alpha \\equiv 0 \\pmod {{\\rm lcm}(m,n)}$ and any Abelian group $\\mathcal{A}$ of order $mn/\\alpha$. %\\cite{ref_CicAus} However, the full characterization of group distance magic Cartesian product of two cycles is still unknown. In the paper we make progress towards the complete solution this problem by proving some necessary conditions. We further prove that for $n$ even the graph $C_{n}\\square C_{n}$ has a $\\Gamma$-distance magic labeling for any Abelian group $\\Gamma$ of order $n^{2}$. Moreover we show that if $m\\neq n$, then there does not exist a $(\\mathbb{Z}_2)^{m+n}$-distance magic labeling of the Cartesian product $C_{2^m} \\square C_{2^{n}}$. We also give necessary and sufficient condition for $C_{m} \\square C_{n}$ with $\\gcd(m,n)=1$ to be $\\Gamma$-distance magic.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGroup distance magic Cartesian product of two cycles (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/341080910_Group_distance_magic_Cartesian_product_of_two_cycles)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; There exists a Γ-distance magic labeling of the Cartesian product C m□C n if and only if ... C m□C n is not a Γ-distance magic graph for any Abelian group Γ of order mn by Theorem 1.5. ... We made some progress towards the full characterization of Abelian groups Γ such that |Γ| = mn and there exists a\n\nProof. When m=n, then we are done by Theorem 1.4. Suppose that m̸= n. Without loss of generality we can assume\n\nthat 2 ≤m<n; then min{f(2 m),f(2 n),gcd(2 m,2 n)} = 2 m−2, whereas lcm(2 m,2 n)=2 n. Observe that the exponent of\n\n(Z 2)m+n is r=2. Therefore 2 r min{f(2 m),f(2 n)} = 2 m<2 n=lcm(m,n). □\n\n4. Conclusion\n\nWe made some progress towards the full characterization of Abelian groups Γ such that |Γ| = mn and there exists a\n\nΓ-distance magic labeling of C m□C n.\n\nWe improved a previous bound for existence of such labeling, showing that if Γ has a cyclic subgroup of order\n\nlcm(m,n)/2, then C m□C n is Γ-distance magic, lowering the bound from lcm(m,n).\n\nOn the other hand, we have shown that groups with an exponent exp(Γ) that is relatively small compared with\n\nlcm(m,n) do not admit such labeling.\n\nSince we found necessary conditions for existence of such labeling (Theorems 3.1 and 3.2), which in some cases were\n\nalso sufficient (Observation 3.5), we post now the following conjecture.\n\nConjecture 4.1. Let Γ be an Abelian group of an even order mn with exponent r . There exists a Γ-distance magic labeling of\n\nthe Cartesian product C m□C n if and only if 2 r min{f(m),f(n),gcd(m,n)} ≥ lcm(m,n).\n\nDeclaration of competing interest\n\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have\n\nappeared to influence the work reported in this paper.\n\n12 S. Cichacz, P. Dyrlaga and D. Froncek / Discrete Mathematics 343 (2020) 111807\n\nReferences\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSylwia Cichacz (https://home.agh.edu.pl/~cichacz/publications.php)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Suchan, Minimu